\title{The official caches are shared by the two modes of the comparison, and an engine with shortcuts cannot know what they hold: under the flag, take the union of the two procedures}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{soundness}
\tag{performance}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-26}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: [the report of package MO-3](rep-MO-3), sections 1 to 3, 6, 14 and 15, and the reviews of its candidates, [from the source](rep-RV-3A) with [its second pass](rep-RV-3A2) and [from the outside](rep-RV-3B). Conditions of the counts: the machine with its default switch set, one worker in file order, the JVM launcher of private builds, the eight corpora; the hostile verdicts are those of leanchecker 4.36.0 in Docker.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The flag was never the problem.} The official kernel sets its eager-reduction flag around one comparison in \code{\verb~|infer_app~} and reads it in two places. The machine had the same bit, set and read in the same places, from its first version. What it lacked was the \em{life} of the official caches: they are shared by the two modes, but they end with the declaration, and the machine's pair table lasts for a session.}
\li{\strong{Keying the table by the mode is wrong.} A pair that a declaration compares in plain mode and then under the flag is answered from the official success cache; a table partitioned by mode computes it under the flag and rejects an export that the official checker accepts.}
\li{\strong{Giving some entries a life works, for the entries}: mark a comparison that passes a point where the two modes may take different branches, and make the entries of a marked comparison local to their declaration. On the eight corpora 0.2 to 2.6 \% of the pair entries are local, at a cost of less than 0.05 \% of the machine's ticks.}
\li{\strong{Reconstructing the official cache does not work.} The official success cache holds every sub-pair that a plain comparison compared. A machine whose plain mode answers by a shortcut never makes those comparisons, so no search of its table and no second run of the declaration finds them: a first candidate that tried both rejected 10 of 58 hand-made and 199 of 3,681 generated exports that the official checker accepts. The same holds for the memo of a reduction that went through the K rule: its comparison can be true in one mode only.}
\li{\strong{What holds instead}: under the flag a pair is true if the eager procedure \em{or} the plain procedure shows it, each failing pair asked again in the other mode at its own level, and two stuck applications of one recursor compared as they stand. Both procedures are sound, so nothing is accepted that is not derivable; nothing is rejected that either can show; and a derivable statement that the official checker rejects through the order of its cache is accepted. On the suite's eager family that is 64 official verdicts and 37 such accepts of 101 rows, where the published mechanism has 63 and 37 with probes, a second run and declines.}
\li{\strong{The comparison of the K rule must be the complete procedure, in both modes.} It is the one place where a true answer changes a reduction, and more reduction can lose a pair: a recursor that the machine reduced against one that nobody can reduce is false, where the official kernel, which left both stuck, compares them argument by argument. The machine's plain mode answered that comparison through a same-head shortcut where the official one is false; every wrong rejection through the K rule that two reviews and the author found, in the published build and in two candidates, came back to it (the published build rejects 527 of 1,220 official accepts of one systematic family). With no shortcut inside the comparison of the K rule and no success read in it that another kind of comparison wrote, all 1,220 are accepted, and the machine's ticks on the eight corpora move by 0.01 \% at most: 0 to 1,413 such comparisons per corpus had used a shortcut.}
\li{\strong{Under the flag, real declarations do nothing eager.} On all eight corpora, 6,156 units of Mathlib among them, no comparison under the flag took a branch that the flag alone allowed, and none was false; the same holds for 1,490 mutants of real declarations made by the second review. Everything above is about hostile exports; a review that only runs real inputs and their mutants cannot see any of it.}
}
